NAME
    tree - list a directory as a tree

SYNOPSIS
    tree [-a] [-d] [path]...

DESCRIPTION
    Walks each path and prints what is under it as a tree, a directory
    per line with the connectors that show which entries have children
    of their own:

        /
        |-- bin/
        |   |-- cat
        |   `-- ls
        `-- etc/
            `-- hostname

    A file given as a path is a tree of one, and several paths are
    printed one after another with a blank line between them. With no
    path at all the current directory is walked.

    Entries are sorted by name, so the same directory prints the same
    way twice. "." and ".." are never listed.

OPTIONS
    -a   list the dot files too, not only the hidden entries that are
         skipped by default
    -d   list the directories and nothing else, which is the shape of
         a filesystem without the files in it

DIAGNOSTICS
    A path that cannot be stat'd, or a directory that cannot be
    opened, is named on standard error and the exit status becomes 1.
    Walking continues over the paths that do work, so one unreadable
    directory does not cost the whole run. An entry whose own stat()
    fails is still listed, marked [cannot stat it], rather than dropped:
    a name is there to be shown.

EXIT STATUS
    0 when every path could be read, 1 when any of them could not.

EXAMPLES
    tree
        the current directory and everything under it

    tree -d /
        the shape of the whole filesystem, directories only

    tree -a /root
        the current directory including .profile and the rest

NOTES
    This is a third-party module: it is built from thams/tree/ and
    lives at /bin/tree, and nothing in the system tree was changed to
    put it there. See thams/README.md in the sources.